---
layout: layouts/base.njk
title: "Message received"
description: "Your message reached Circulation Studio. Here is what happens next."
permalink: /thank-you/index.html

# NOINDEX, AND IT IS A REAL PAGE ANYWAY.
#
# A confirmation page has nothing to rank for and should not compete with
# /contact/ for anything, so it carries the directive. It reaches base.njk
# through pageNoindex, the same computed value /library/ and the fixtures use,
# so it is out of sitemap.xml by the same mechanism rather than a second one.
#
# NOT eleventyExcludeFromCollections, unlike /yelp-map/ and 404.njk. Those two
# are excluded because nothing should ever list them. This page is a normal
# page that happens to be noindexed, and being in collections.all is how the
# build's dead-link check knows the URL exists. The Function redirects here, and
# a confirmation page that 404s is worse than no confirmation at all.
noindex: true

# THE ILLUSTRATION. Above the fold and the only visual on the page, so it is
# eager and preloaded rather than lazy. preloadImage is what base.njk preloads
# AND what the <img> takes as its src, so the two cannot drift; the dimensions
# are the file's true intrinsic size, measured from the file, and they are what
# reserves the space.
preloadImage: cs-img-homing-pigeon-portrait.webp
heroWidth: 800
heroHeight: 1069
heroAlt: >-
  A homing pigeon in flight over a pale paper field, with survey annotation
  lines and a single red bar marking its course
---
{% from "components/container.njk" import container %}
{% from "components/email.njk" import email %}

{#
  THE CONFIRMATION PAGE, AND WHY IT IS A PAGE RATHER THAN A QUERY STRING.

  The first design for this was a 303 to /contact/?sent=1 with the message
  swapped in. That does not work on this host and the reason is worth recording
  next to the thing it caused: Cloudflare Pages serves static HTML, so
  /contact/?sent=1 returns bytes identical to /contact/. Nothing on the server
  reads the query string and nothing in the file reacts to it. A visitor with
  JavaScript off would land back on an empty form with no confirmation and no
  way to know their message had sent, which is the exact failure the four
  completeness prompts exist to avoid at the other end of the same form.

  So the no-JavaScript path gets a real URL with real bytes.

  IT COSTS NOTHING, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS EASY. /thank-you is already a live
  indexed URL on the current site: verified 2026-08-17 as HTTP 200 with a
  self-referencing canonical. Building it keeps the path warm through cutover
  instead of redirecting it away and back.

  WITH JAVASCRIPT ON, NOBODY SEES THIS. main.js intercepts the submit and swaps
  the panel in place, so the visitor never leaves /contact/. This is the
  fallback, and it has to read as a finished page rather than as one.

  THE RESPONSE WINDOW IS STATED ONCE, HERE. Not in the form, not in the email.
  A promise repeated in three places is a promise that will disagree with itself.

  THE OVERLAP FOLLOWS .cs-net-hero, mirrored. Same mechanism, not a second one:
  a relative frame, the copy absolutely positioned across the picture's edge by
  a fraction of its own width, and the whole overlay abandoned at 1120 in favour
  of stacking. The difference is only which side the picture sits on.

  NO PLATE AND NO EXTENDED RULE. The illustration's ground is cream and the
  page's is not, so the copy does cross a change of paper on its way onto the
  picture. Backing the composition with a matched cream plate was tried and
  rejected: it turns a small tonal step inside one sentence into a large panel
  across the page. The picture also carries a dashed annotation at 48.55% of its
  height, measured at ink .32 against --border-hairline's .14, and continuing it
  would put a second, heavier dashed treatment on the site for one page.
#}

<div class="cs-thanks">
  {% call container() %}
    <div class="cs-thanks__frame">
      {#- Eager and fetchpriority high: it is the LCP element. Explicit width and
          height are the file's intrinsic size, so the box is reserved before the
          bytes arrive and the copy over it does not jump. -#}
      <img class="cs-thanks__figure"
           src="{{ site.cdn }}{{ preloadImage }}"
           alt="{{ heroAlt }}"
           width="{{ heroWidth }}" height="{{ heroHeight }}"
           loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async">

      {#- THE SURVEY RULE, CARRIED UP OUT OF THE PICTURE. The vertical that
          runs through the bird reaches the top edge of the file at 76.75%
          across; this continues it into the paper above, so the survey grid
          reads as passing behind the picture rather than being contained by it.

          The site's hairline, not the picture's line: the drawn one is heavier
          and this vocabulary has one weight. It registers with it rather than
          imitating it. See the page CSS for why the offset is 88.375% and not
          76.75%. -#}
      <div class="cs-rule cs-rule--pigeon-v" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      {#- AND THE HORIZONTAL, out of the picture's right edge into the gutter.
          The line crossing the middle of the picture at 48.55% is the only
          horizontal in the file that reaches an edge, so it is the only one
          there is anything to continue. It runs right, into empty margin,
          because left would cross the confirmation. -#}
      <div class="cs-rule cs-rule--pigeon-h" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      {#- WHAT OVERLAPS IS THE CONFIRMATION AND NOTHING ELSE. The copy crosses
          the picture's left edge by --copy-on-image, so only that strip sits on
          the illustration. Measured over x 0 to 25% of the file, the picture is
          entirely clear of dark content from 10% to 45% of its height, which is
          the band this block is positioned into. The two follow-up lines are
          below the plate rather than in it: they would have pushed the block
          past 48% of the height, which is where the annotation line crosses,
          and type over a dashed rule is unreadable. -#}
      <div class="cs-thanks__copy">
        <p class="cs-eyebrow">Contact</p>
        <h1 class="cs-thanks__title">Message received</h1>
        <p class="cs-thanks__lede">
          It is with us, and one of us will read it rather than a queue. Expect
          a reply within two working days.
        </p>

        {#- THE FOLLOW-UPS SIT IN THE SAME COLUMN, and they are narrower on
            purpose. They were briefly their own container below the picture,
            which produced two defects at once: 590px of empty page under the
            copy, and a second left edge, because a container-aligned block does
            not line up with a column inset to reach the picture. One column
            with one left edge fixes both.

            THE NARROW CAP IS WHAT KEEPS THEM OFF THE PICTURE. Only the lede
            above needs to reach across; these do not, and they sit lower, where
            the annotation line and the bird's wing are. Capped short of the
            picture's left edge, they stay on paper for their whole length. -#}
        <div class="cs-thanks__after">
          <p>
            If it is quicker to talk, the studio number is
            <a href="{{ site.nap.telephoneHref }}">{{ site.nap.telephone }}</a>,
            and you can reach us directly at
            {{ email(site.nap.emailUser, site.nap.emailDomain) }}.
          </p>

          {#- The Yelp Hub rather than the home page. Somebody who has just
              written in about their listing is closer to wanting the writing
              than to wanting the front door, and /library/ is a placeholder
              until the library opens. -#}
          <p>
            In the meantime, the <a class="cs-link" href="/yelp/">Yelp Hub</a>
            carries everything we have published on Yelp advertising.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  {% endcall %}
</div>
